# Auto commit and push

When you finish making code changes for the user (not for question-only or review-only tasks):

1. Run `git status` and `git diff` to review what changed.
2. Stage only relevant files — never commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, tokens).
3. Commit with a concise message (1–2 sentences, focus on why). Match repo style (`feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, etc.).
4. Push: `git push origin main`
5. Briefly tell the user the commit hash and that push succeeded (or report push errors).

Do this at the end of the same turn, without waiting for the user to ask again.

Skip auto commit/push when:
- The user explicitly says not to commit
- There are no file changes
- The task was read-only (explaining, reviewing, answering questions)
